# Paths reqmap should not scan when invoked with --code pointed at the repo
# root (this repo's own CI + pre-commit hook only, via `--code ..`/`--code .`).
# The narrow default invocation (cd plugin/ && reqmap.py gate, no --code) never
# reads this file — it reads plugin/.reqmapignore instead. The two are kept in
# sync deliberately, NOT merged into one relocated file: moving plugin's own
# .reqmapignore to the repo root would silently stop excluding these same
# paths for the narrow invocation, since load_ignore() checks reqs_dir then
# code_root, and code_root differs between the two invocation styles.

plugin/scripts/_map_viewer.html

plugin/skills/excalidraw-diagram/SKILL.md
plugin/skills/excalidraw-diagram/SKILL.universal.md
plugin/skills/excalidraw-diagram/examples/*.py
plugin/skills/excalidraw-diagram/references/excalidraw_format.md

plugin/skills/requirement-manager/SKILL.md
plugin/skills/requirement-manager/SKILL.universal.md

plugin/docs/superpowers/specs/*.md

# Session working artifacts (specs/plans), gitignored, not capability implementations.
docs/superpowers/**

# Isolated subagent worktrees (gitignored since 13634a0). Each holds a FULL second
# copy of this tree, so a widened local scan double-counts every member and reports
# the copies' README/docs illustration ids (AUTH-LOGIN-001, LOGIN-001, AC-1) as
# dangling-tag ERRORS. CI checks out a clean tree and never sees them, so without
# this the local gate and CI disagree — exactly the divergence the gate exists to
# prevent, and it would bake .worktrees/ paths into a locally regenerated map.
.worktrees/**

# Consilium deliberation artifacts (gitignored, .gitignore:3). Same class as the
# worktrees above, and it bites harder: .consilium/FEEDBACK.html carries a real
# `generated-from:` tag, so a map regenerated on a machine that has run Consilium
# gains a member the CI checkout cannot reproduce — `map --check` then fails on CI
# for a file that is not in the repo. Committed generated artifacts must depend only
# on tracked files.
.consilium/**

# app/ (Vite+React viewer source) IS scanned since 2026-09-02: app/src carries
# per-file `implements:` tags (REQ-VIEWER-007, REQ-SEARCH-036, REQ-TRANSLATE-044)
# and app/scripts/ssr-smoke.jsx a `tested-by:`. Only its build outputs and local
# caches are excluded — all gitignored, so CI never sees them, and the bundles
# would otherwise re-report every tag from the sources they were built from.
app/dist/**
app/dist-viewer/**
app/.vite/**
app/scripts/.ssr-smoke.bundle.*

# Local-only caches and session artifacts (all gitignored). CI never sees them,
# so leaving them scannable makes `next`/`coverage`/`health` report untagged
# files that do not exist in the repo.
.pytest_cache/**
*/.pytest_cache/**
.ruff_cache/**
.superpowers/**
# diagrams/: regenerable Excalidraw outputs (gitignored) + a README. No members.
diagrams/**

# docs/map.html is the published render of the SAME generated viewer template
# excluded above (plugin/scripts/_map_viewer.html) — it inlines this repo's own
# _map.json, which embeds CORE-SCAN-002's prose (its own "implements: LOGIN-001"
# illustration and its "AC-1" acceptance-criterion label) as plain JS/JSON text.
# Once code_root reaches docs/, that inlined text matches TAG_RE and reads as a
# dangling tag pointing at a requirement that doesn't exist. Never a hand-authored
# member; excluded for the same reason as the template it's rendered from.
docs/map.html
# docs/architecture.html is a reviewed, hand-authored diagram (produced by the
# excalidraw-diagram skill) that renders a code-editor mockup illustrating the
# tagging syntax from README.md's own walkthrough, using the same fictional
# "AUTH-LOGIN-001" example id — not a real membership tag. Unlike README.md's
# fenced ```python block, the diagram's syntax-highlighted markup isn't inside a
# backtick fence the prose scanner recognizes, so the illustration is excluded by
# path instead. This file already carries real generated-from: tags to other
# requirements; excluding it here is a scope decision for this pass (matching
# app/'s), not a claim that its content should never be drift-checked.
docs/architecture.html
